    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\nLocality derivation now prefers the postcode, falling back to the city only when the postcode is absent or in no known range.\n\n## Where the logic went (and why)\n\n- **`PostcodeRule.regionForPostcode(String)`** \u2014 added. `PostcodeRule` already owns the region\u2192postcode-range table (`REGION_POSTCODES`), so the inverse lookup (postcode \u2192 region) belongs there too, reusing the same ranges and the same `FOUR_DIGITS` pattern rather than duplicating them. Returns `null` for an absent/malformed/out-of-range postcode.\n\n- **`LocalityResolver`** \u2014 the derivation authority. I split `resolve` into two methods with clear responsibilities:\n  - `resolve(city, postcode)` \u2014 the new postcode-first entry point: consult `PostcodeRule.regionForPostcode`, then fall back to the city.\n  - `resolve(city)` \u2014 retained as the city-only lookup, still used by `PostcodeRule.isValid` (which must validate a postcode against the *city's* region, so it deliberately stays city-based \u2014 otherwise postcodes would validate against themselves).\n\n- **`OwnerMapper.resolveLocality`** \u2014 now passes `owner.getPostcode()` alongside the city.\n\nNo class became a catch-all; each table stayed with its owner, and no ranges were copied. This returns the same region for known cities (e.g. Sydney + 2000 \u2192 NSW) but disambiguates same-named cities via the postcode (e.g. an unknown city + 3000 \u2192 VIC), matching both new acceptance tests.",
